#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128788 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1091c5-d81a-565f-a281-b2fb0d00c973" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Not Centered at the Origin"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e46cfe46-965d-5c28-90ed-060ec39751a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12376" :question/problem "Given that $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x^2 + (y+2)^2 \\leq 4\\right\\}$, express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D e^{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}A$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$. Expanding the region gives $$x^2+(y+2)^2\\le4\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad r^2+4r\\sin\\theta\\le0.$$ The disk lies below the origin, so $\\pi\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$ and $0\\le r\\le-4\\sin\\theta$. Since $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, $$\\iint_De^{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\,dA=\\int_\\pi^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{-4\\sin\\theta}re^r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3ae6c85-14bd-57ad-b8cc-1cebb53683b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7346" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}\\int _{0}^{\\sin 2\\theta }rdrd\\theta  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}[r^{2}]_{0}^{\\sin 2\\theta }d\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sin^{2} 2\\theta d\\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sin^{2} 2\\theta d\\theta  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1 - \\cos 4\\theta )d\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}1 - \\cos 4\\theta d\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[\\theta  - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin 4\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8} - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) - (0 - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (0))] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8} - \\frac{2}{8}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{32}(\\pi  - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128952 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "059deff9-dfa3-5c3e-88b1-194af41d3ed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7347" :question/problem "The region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2030/Source/Images/e61cb3c40b449c031c9d9074f8983b94.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows the first-quadrant quarter-disk of radius $1$, so $0\\le r\\le1$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$. Substitute $y=r\\sin\\theta$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$, and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$. Thus $$\\iint_Dy\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,dA=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^1(r\\sin\\theta)r\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^1r^3\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128953 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f31dd085-70fa-5eed-adf9-89fb0e6654e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7348" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2030/Source/Images/3a076157edac08be902902303455d72d.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\iint _{D}15xy^{2}dA &= \\iint _{\\Delta }15r\\cos \\theta  \\cdot (r\\sin \\theta )^{2}rdrd\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}15r^{4}\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta drd\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta \\int _{0}^{2}5r^{4}drd\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta [r^{5}]_{0}^{2}d\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta (2^{5} - 0)d\\theta  \\\\\n&= 32\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta d\\theta  \\\\\n&= 32[\\sin^{3} \\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\\n&= 32(\\sin^{3} (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) - \\sin^{3} (0)) \\\\\n&= 32(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0814c2f3-5c4f-52f2-9d07-fcbe7628adcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127185} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf61cf4-b9f6-5fa0-8963-c795887195e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127186} {:answer/id #uuid "10cd9673-7ed3-5bc4-aa7c-30697030263a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127187} {:answer/id #uuid "25feec3f-f21a-5584-a043-d339ec59f3fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127188} {:answer/id #uuid "2c2854c9-7c6e-5474-9bd3-30f61413e3ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cf61cf4-b9f6-5fa0-8963-c795887195e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127186} :answer-field/id #uuid "609a2d8a-e7b9-558b-a3e7-632287f13277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fcddcef-1856-5848-a572-712ca0d53c78" :question/math-academy-id "q-38031" :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{\\sin \\theta }2r\\cos \\theta drd\\theta $." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Integrate first with respect to $r$: $$\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^{\\sin\\theta}2r\\cos\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^2\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta.$$ Let $u=\\sin\\theta$, so $du=\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta$ and the bounds become $0,1$. The result is $\\int_0^1u^2\\,du=1/3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8992174c-bcfb-56fd-9002-d0ead855b09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{- \\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{1}r^{2}\\sin (2\\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127191} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6648f9-e38e-5041-9150-1ddf3096e626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}r^{2}\\sin (2\\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127192} {:answer/id #uuid "be95ae45-1eb1-57d4-a80d-de27fdc7c098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{- \\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}r^{3}\\sin (2\\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127193} {:answer/id #uuid "db160bd8-2bdf-56f2-8157-c73f8b6ea964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{- \\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}r^{2}\\sin (2\\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127194} {:answer/id #uuid "23cbcc85-b4bd-549a-9dc8-0df925ff8b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}r^{3}\\sin (2\\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be95ae45-1eb1-57d4-a80d-de27fdc7c098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{- \\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}r^{3}\\sin (2\\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127193} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a30920b-68a6-56bf-9532-2f30e22b210a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "695450ef-6209-5a8e-874a-0e67f88a5889" :question/math-academy-id "q-38076" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2030/Source/Images/q-38076.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n$\\iint _{D}2xydA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The diagram is the right half of the radius-$2$ disk: $0\\le r\\le2$ and $-\\pi/2\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$. Also $2xy=2r^2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta=r^2\\sin(2\\theta)$. Including the factor $r$ from $dA$ gives $$\\iint_D2xy\\,dA=\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^2r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2387972-198a-5fa7-b04f-e9bee3ff4d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127221} {:answer/id #uuid "334b907f-8925-5260-9f16-d8f5f3d9ba6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127222} {:answer/id #uuid "35cb5d86-9eb9-521d-8abb-e286823bac5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127223} {:answer/id #uuid "66e0b1f7-07f8-507d-8326-af46a123cdab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127224} {:answer/id #uuid "73d74f9f-365d-5075-8c13-d83e404f595c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "334b907f-8925-5260-9f16-d8f5f3d9ba6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127222} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb8ffd0-ede6-59bb-8cc0-fb5c889e3362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a604ede7-8a36-5960-82df-176ca647b425" :question/math-academy-id "q-38140" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 2x + y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y \\ge 0$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The upper half-disk has $0\\le r\\le2$, $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$. Hence $$\\iint_D(2x+y)\\,dA=\\int_0^\\pi\\int_0^2r^2(2\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\frac83\\int_0^\\pi(2\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,d\\theta.$$ The cosine integral is zero and the sine integral is $2$, giving $16/3$."}]}
